<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<form name="frmHH_11" align="client" theme="dark">
	<!--
		Vampire tab (SPEC I16, 51st round). This file is a SHELL: it owns the tabControl and
		nothing else. The content lives one file per area because the descriptions run to
		hundreds of book entries, and HH.7 already spends 397 KB on 43 numina - one .lfm for
		all of it would be megabytes (SPEC C).

		    Vampire ─┬─ Disciplines            → HH.12
		             ├─ Blood Sorcery: Paths   → HH.13
		             └─ Blood Sorcery: Rituals → HH.14

		THREE SIBLING TABS, one tabControl (SPEC V188, 54th round). It used to be two
		tabControls, the second nested inside a `Blood Sorcery` tab, which is the shape the
		tree above is really describing. The nested control drew its tabs but never their
		labels: the strip came up blank, so the sub-tabs were three unmarked click targets
		(SPEC R49). Rather than keep probing why a tabControl misbehaves inside a <tab>, the
		grouping moved into the titles - `Blood Sorcery: Paths` and `Blood Sorcery: Rituals`
		say the same thing a parent tab said, in a control the host is known to render.

		The tabControl and all three tabs are authored HERE, in the XML, never built by Lua
		(SPEC V188): a sheet whose script did not run still opens with the structure, the same
		way the root tabControl does. Default is the first tab, Disciplines.

		The tab is hidden unless the storyteller ticks the flag, which is still the field
		`stShowDisciplines` even though the tab now reads `Vampire` - a field cannot be renamed
		after release (SPEC V2/V172), so the name stays and the label moves.

		Still declares no `field` of its own (SPEC V90), and carries NO theme backdrop.

		It used to. That is SPEC B41: the backdrop is align="client", the tabControl is
		align="client", and two siblings claiming one client rect means the loser is not drawn.
		applyTheme turns the paper visible on every load - Modern Nights ships paper_present.png
		- so a stretched image sat over the page area and the sub-tabs opened onto nothing.

		The backdrop belongs to the form that carries CONTENT, not to the tab (SPEC V56, V190).
		This file carries none, so the paper lives in HH.12, HH.13 and HH.14 instead, one per
		scrollBox, exactly the arrangement HH.7 uses. What is left here is the tabControl alone,
		which is the arrangement the root form uses for its own tabControl.
	-->
	<tabControl name="tabsVamp" align="client">
		<tab name="tabDisc" title="Disciplines">
			<import file="HH.12.lfm"/>
		</tab>
		<tab name="tabPaths" title="Blood Sorcery: Paths">
			<import file="HH.13.lfm"/>
		</tab>
		<tab name="tabRituals" title="Blood Sorcery: Rituals">
			<import file="HH.14.lfm"/>
		</tab>
	</tabControl>
</form>
